// Durability contracts for recovery-critical state.
//
// Why this exists separately from reliability-gates.jsonc: that manifest disciplines *claims* —
// it will not let a gate assert protection without a real test, command, and passed evidence run.
// It never inspects source, so a subsystem can carry a formal invariant, eleven assertion refs and
// a green evidence run while its writes have no durability barrier at all. That is not
// hypothetical: terminal-history.daemon-checkpoint-session-isolation described "durable-history
// checkpoints" three times, and the checkpoint write had no fsync anywhere in src/main/daemon.
//
// A durability contract answers a question no gate asks: if this state is lost, what breaks, and
// what does the write owe as a result? Tiers exist because durability must be proportional —
// forcing fsync on regenerable caches buys nothing and costs an I/O storm.
//
// tiers:
//   crash-durable   The state is the only copy of something the user would notice losing, and the
//                   pointer naming it is itself fsynced. Writes must route through
//                   src/main/durable-file-write.ts and carry a syscall-order proof.
//   atomic-only     Readers must never observe a torn file, but losing the newest write to power
//                   loss is acceptable because a higher tier can reconstruct it.
//   regenerable     Recomputable from a source of truth. No barrier owed.
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "policy": {
    "tiers": ["crash-durable", "atomic-only", "regenerable"],
    // Why proof is required only at the top tier: an ordering claim is the one property that cannot
    // be read off the implementation, because an fsync moved after the rename still fsyncs a file.
    "proofRequiredForTiers": ["crash-durable"],
    "durablePrimitive": "src/main/durable-file-write.ts",
    // Ratcheted, not reported: config/durability-unclassified-baseline.txt grandfathers the
    // publishers that predate this gate and may only shrink, so legacy debt stays visible while a
    // newly added unclassified publisher fails the gate. "There were already some" is not an
    // argument a future change can make.
    "unclassifiedWriterPolicy": "ratchet"
  },
  "contracts": [
    {
      "id": "persistence.profile-data-file",
      "state": "orca-data.json and its .bak ring — workspace session, terminal layouts, open files",
      // Two publishers, and the gate holds both to the tier. The transfer writer was a bare
      // write+rename while this contract read as satisfied — a contract names state, not one file,
      // and its guarantee is only as strong as its weakest publisher.
      "writer": [
        "src/main/persistence/loading-store/store.ts",
        "src/main/orca-profiles/profile-project-state-file.ts"
      ],
      "tier": "crash-durable",
      "lostIf": "A power loss between rename and flush leaves a zero-length profile, and the user reopens Orca to an empty workspace.",
      "provenBy": [
        "src/main/persistence-async-write-syscalls.test.ts",
        "src/main/orca-profiles/profile-project-state-durability.test.ts"
      ],
      "knownGaps": [
        "The .bak ring bounds loss at roughly one hour, so the contract is 'no total loss', not 'no loss'.",
        "Only loading-store rotates the .bak ring; a profile transfer replaces the file with no backup refresh, so a transfer is the one publish with no recovery slot behind it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "daemon.terminal-history-checkpoint",
      "state": "checkpoint.json per daemon session, plus the generation-headered output.log it supersedes",
      "writer": "src/main/daemon/terminal-history-session-writer.ts",
      "tier": "crash-durable",
      "lostIf": "The checkpoint is the sole copy once the log is reset. An unsynced publish can expose a zero-length checkpoint whose increments were already discarded, losing the session's scrollback across a crash the daemon was built to survive.",
      "provenBy": ["src/main/daemon/terminal-history-checkpoint-durability.test.ts"],
      "knownGaps": [
        "The log reset is ordered after the checkpoint fsync, but the reset itself is not fsynced; a loss there costs the reset, not the checkpoint."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "terminal.scrollback-snapshot",
      "state": "terminal-scrollback/<ref>.bin, referenced by scrollbackRefsByLeafId in the profile data file",
      "writer": "src/main/terminal-scrollback-snapshots.ts",
      "tier": "crash-durable",
      "lostIf": "The referencing profile file is fsynced and the blob was not, so a durable pointer can outlive its referent. The reader returns an empty string for a zero-length snapshot, so the loss surfaces as empty scrollback rather than as a failure.",
      "provenBy": ["src/main/terminal-scrollback-snapshot-durability.test.ts"],
      "knownGaps": [
        "The reader still cannot distinguish a missing snapshot from a genuinely empty one; see shared/terminal-snapshot-unavailability.ts for the vocabulary the live wire path already uses."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "browser.session-meta",
      "state": "browser-session-meta.json — browser profiles, default source, pending cookie imports",
      "writer": "src/main/browser/browser-session-meta-store.ts",
      "tier": "atomic-only",
      "lostIf": "Losing the newest write costs one profile edit. The severe failure here was never durability: a read-modify-write seeded from a failed read published an erasure of every profile, so the contract this file owes is a classified read, not an fsync.",
      "provenBy": ["src/main/browser/browser-session-meta-transient-read-failure.test.ts"],
      "knownGaps": [
        "There is no backup ring for this file, so a corrupt-quarantine is the only recovery path."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "integrations.credential-file",
      "state": "Per-integration token files (Linear, Jira), safeStorage-encrypted where a keyring exists",
      "writer": "src/main/integration-credential-file.ts",
      "tier": "crash-durable",
      "lostIf": "The user is silently signed out of the integration and must re-authenticate. An empty file reads as 'missing' by design, so a torn publish degrades straight to logged-out with no error.",
      "provenBy": ["src/main/integration-credential-durability.test.ts"],
      "knownGaps": [
        "The barrier is hand-rolled rather than routed through durable-file-write, and it stays that way deliberately. Consolidation was attempted once the primitive learned to remove its own temp, and reverted: bitbucket/credential-store.test.ts injects failures at writeSync and unlinkSync to hold the STA-3941 guarantee, so replacing the explicit short-write loop deletes the boundary those regression tests assert against. This writer is specialized, not duplicated.",
        "The containing directory is not fsynced, so the rename itself is durable only where the filesystem orders it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ssh-relay.endpoint-credential",
      "state": "Relay endpoint shared secret, written on the REMOTE host by an SSH-executed script",
      "writer": "src/main/ssh/ssh-relay-endpoint-credential.ts",
      "tier": "atomic-only",
      "lostIf": "The paired client cannot authenticate to the relay endpoint until a redeploy regenerates the secret. The value is freshly random per deploy, so it is replaceable rather than irrecoverable.",
      "knownGaps": [
        "The local durable primitive cannot apply: the bytes are written by node -e or PowerShell on the remote host, not by this process.",
        "The two platform paths are asymmetric. Windows opens the temp with FileOptions::WriteThrough, which bypasses the cache; the POSIX script does writeFileSync then renameSync with no fsync. Only the Windows path currently survives remote power loss.",
        "This file matched the publisher scan on the rename text inside its remote script string rather than on a local call."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "codex-accounts.managed-credential",
      "state": "Per-account auth.json and .credentials.json for every managed Codex and Claude account, plus the migration completion markers beside them",
      "writer": "src/main/codex-accounts/fs-utils.ts",
      "tier": "crash-durable",
      "lostIf": "The user is silently signed out of a managed account. An empty auth.json reads as 'no credential' by design, so a torn publish degrades straight to logged-out with nothing to explain it — and because the legacy migration gates its retry on marker existence alone, a marker that outlived its credential makes that state terminal.",
      "provenBy": [
        "src/main/codex-accounts/managed-auth-durability.test.ts",
        "src/main/codex-accounts/legacy-shared-auth-migration.test.ts"
      ],
      "knownGaps": [
        "The barrier is hand-rolled rather than routed through durable-file-write, deliberately: these publications need the Windows ACL recovery and the EPERM/EBUSY rename backoff (#1507) that the shared synchronous primitive has no timer to carry.",
        "The containing directory is not fsynced, so the rename is durable only where the filesystem orders it. The migration proof asserts barrier-before-publish for each file, which is the leg that does not depend on that."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "codex.config-toml-trust",
      "state": "The user's own ~/.codex/config.toml — their comments, key order and every unrelated Codex setting — plus the hook trusted_hash and project trust_level Orca edits into it",
      "writer": "src/main/codex/config-toml-trust.ts",
      "tier": "crash-durable",
      "lostIf": "A torn publish leaves the file zero-length, which reads back as a legitimately empty TOML document rather than as damage. The next trust edit then rewrites the user's whole configuration as trust entries alone, and Codex silently stops firing hooks or re-prompts for project trust.",
      "provenBy": ["src/main/codex/config-toml-trust-durability.test.ts"],
      "knownGaps": [
        "Zero-length and intentionally-empty remain indistinguishable at the read boundary, because an empty config.toml is valid TOML. The defence is therefore the barrier that stops Orca creating that state, plus refusing to roll .bak forward from an empty file — not a read-side rejection that would break legitimately empty configs.",
        "The read-modify-write holds no lock and races the Codex CLI's own /hooks writer; an idempotent reinstall repairs a lost update."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "plugins.install-state",
      "state": "plugins.lock.json, each plugin's current-content pointer, install provenance and the marketplace kill-list",
      "writer": "src/main/plugins/plugin-atomic-file-write.ts",
      "tier": "atomic-only",
      "lostIf": "A plugin drops out of the lockfile that install and update paths consult, so it can be reinstalled or skipped. Recoverable: recoverPluginLockfile rebuilds the lock by scanning the plugins directory, which is the real source of truth.",
      "knownGaps": [
        "Distinct from durable-file-write, not a duplicate of it: this publisher is async-only and carries a Windows EPERM/EACCES/EBUSY rename backoff (#1507) that the synchronous primitive has no timer to park on. Merging the two would force one contract to adopt the other's cost — and do not add a third.",
        "An earlier revision of this contract claimed a transient pointer-read failure drops a plugin from the reconstructed lock. That was wrong and is retracted: recoverPluginLockfile seeds from the existing lock and only ever assigns, so the failure path preserves the entry, and the write is gated on recovered.changed. The authoritative record is the immutable per-hash provenance file the lock is rebuilt from, and the lock is not on the consent or activation path."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ui.active-view-preference",
      "state": "The last active view, persisted beside the profile data file",
      "writer": "src/main/active-view-preference.ts",
      "tier": "atomic-only",
      "lostIf": "Orca reopens on the previously stored view instead of the newest one. No user content is involved and the next view change rewrites it.",
      "knownGaps": [
        "There is no barrier here at all, which is deliberate at this tier: an fsync on every view toggle would cost more than the one preference it protects."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ui.window-topology",
      "state": "ui.windowTopology inside the profile data file — which workspace windows were open, which project each owned, and where each sat",
      "writer": "src/main/window/window-topology-persistence.ts",
      "tier": "atomic-only",
      "lostIf": "A relaunch opens one default window instead of the set the user had. The projects, terminals and layouts are untouched — they live in the session this file already carries — so the loss is the arrangement, not the work.",
      "provenBy": [
        "src/main/window/window-topology.test.ts",
        "src/main/window/window-topology-persistence.test.ts"
      ],
      "knownGaps": [
        "It is a field of orca-data.json, so in practice it receives that file's crash-durable barrier — stronger than this tier owes. The tier states what the state is worth, not what the host file happens to give it; if the topology ever moves to a file of its own, atomic-only is the guarantee that must survive the move.",
        "The dangerous shape here was never the barrier, it was collapse-into-empty. A quit closes every window one at a time, so the last close is the write that would publish an empty set over a good one, and an unreadable value returned as [] would do the same on the next publish. Both are refused in window-topology.ts rather than in the writer, which is why the proof is a pure test and not a syscall trace.",
        "No completion marker gates the restore: the plan is re-derived from the recorded entries on every launch, so there is no 'topology restored' flag that could become durable ahead of the state it certifies."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
